      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Given the equations of the two lines are \\( y = mx + 4 \\) and \\( y = 3x + b \\), and they intersect at the point \\((6, 10)\\), we can substitute \\( x = 6 \\) and \\( y = 10 \\) into both equations to find the values of \\( m \\) and \\( b \\).\n\nFirst, substitute \\((6, 10)\\) into the equation \\( y = mx + 4 \\):\n\\[ 10 = 6m + 4 \\]\nSubtract 4 from both sides:\n\\[ 6 = 6m \\]\nDivide both sides by 6:\n\\[ m = 1 \\]\n\nNext, substitute \\((6, 10)\\) into the equation \\( y = 3x + b \\):\n\\[ 10 = 3(6) + b \\]\n\\[ 10 = 18 + b \\]\nSubtract 18 from both sides:\n\\[ -8 = b \\]\n\nSo, we have \\( m = 1 \\) and \\( b = -8 \\). Therefore, the value of \\( b + m \\) is:\n\\[ b + m = -8 + 1 = -7 \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{-7} \\]",
